WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.747
FULL OUTER JOIN is especially useful for reconciliation.

00:00:03.747 --> 00:00:12.072
Reconciliation means comparing two sources and making sure we can see matches as well as records that exist in

00:00:12.072 --> 00:00:12.392
only one source.

00:00:12.392 --> 00:00:18.982
In our example, the patient master is the official table, while the staging table represents imported data.

00:00:18.982 --> 00:00:23.877
We want to see three possibilities: a patient that exists in both tables,

00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:30.182
a patient that exists only in the official master, and a staging row that has no official patient.

00:00:30.182 --> 00:00:35.202
This same idea appears in finance when comparing two transaction systems,

00:00:35.202 --> 00:00:39.197
in human resources when comparing payroll with employee records,

00:00:39.197 --> 00:00:43.982
or in education when comparing enrollment systems with learning-platform accounts.

00:00:43.982 --> 00:00:50.307
The CASE expression in the query helps turn those situations into readable labels such as matched,

00:00:50.307 --> 00:00:53.707
missing from staging, or missing from master.

00:00:53.707 --> 00:01:00.502
COALESCE can also help when the identifier may come from either table depending on which side is unmatched.

00:01:00.502 --> 00:01:04.982
FULL OUTER JOIN is powerful because it does not throw away evidence.

00:01:04.982 --> 00:01:11.035
When you are doing data-quality work, unmatched rows are often the rows that deserve the most attention.
